WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.560
Ever played a game with crazy amounts of&nbsp;
stuff, yet running so smoothly it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.760
like magic? Behind that buttery smooth&nbsp;
performance is more than just powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.840
hardware. I started as a graphics engineer&nbsp;
almost 20 years ago, and since then I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:20.040
always been fascinated with optimizations,&nbsp;
but one stands out as both incredibly simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:25.600
yet the reason WHY it works is more complex&nbsp;
than most people realise. Let’s examine one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.880
of the most effective optimization techniques&nbsp;
available, and understand it to the same level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.880
of depth that experienced engineers do.
Let’s start with a little world and we’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.400
add a buncha stuff to it. This will serve&nbsp;
as kinda our testbed to show things off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.560
so we’ll keep it reasonably simple.
Let’s go ahead and add an object to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:50.440
the world here, so we’ve got our player and&nbsp;
this object. The idea is relatively simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:56.320
this object will have several versions, each&nbsp;
being progressively simpler. So here’s the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.880
complex or detailed version of the object,&nbsp;
and we can sort of pan the camera down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:08.240
line looking at the simpler and simpler versions.&nbsp;
These will be called levels of detail, or LOD.
&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:13.320
What we’re going to do is, when we place one of&nbsp;
these objects in the world, we’ll start it nearby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:19.040
for convenience, and the idea is as the object&nbsp;
gets further and further away from the camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:25.000
we cycle through the different levels of detail&nbsp;
for that object. So we’re a bit further away, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.720
switch to a lower level of detail. A bit further,&nbsp;
and we can do that again, switch to an even lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:38.760
level of detail, that sorta thing. How many levels&nbsp;
of detail games use is a bit of a tossup, since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:43.680
authoring them takes artist time, you may only&nbsp;
have enough budget to build a high and low level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.240
of detail, or you may have money to burn and you&nbsp;
can build a half dozen versions of the asset.
&nbsp;

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:54.320
This brings us to a really awesome optimization&nbsp;
that’s used quite a bit, called imposters. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:58.760
many people, this is just another level&nbsp;
of detail, or a whole other technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:02.880
I mean it doesn’t really matter.
Let’s take these 2 objects here in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.880
distance. From this viewpoint, what are the&nbsp;
differences between those 2? Not a whole lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.880
but as we move forward, we can see that&nbsp;
something is kinda off with one of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:18.560
This one looks perfectly normal, but the other&nbsp;
one is clearly just a picture that’s rotating with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.640
us. I can freeze it so that we can walk around it&nbsp;
and see that this is just a floating texture. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:25.840
is a billboard or imposter, and from far enough&nbsp;
away, you really can’t tell the difference.
&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.520
With a little bit of extra work, these imposters&nbsp;
can be really advanced, you can render out normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:35.960
maps along with the diffuse, you can render them&nbsp;
from multiple viewpoints and blend between them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:41.680
giving you a highly versatile and incredibly&nbsp;
low cost stand in for the original asset.
&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:47.160
This scene here from my previous grass video&nbsp;
features something like 50k trees of various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:53.000
types, and it’s trivially cheap to render&nbsp;
due to most of them being imposters.
&nbsp;

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:57.000
The question now is, why does&nbsp;
this technique work so well? 
&nbsp;

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.520
The intuitive answer is that these models&nbsp;
have fewer vertices, and so then it sorta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.800
makes sense that having fewer vertices equals&nbsp;
doing less work on the gpu. But pressed for more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.920
information than that, this is kinda where&nbsp;
the explanation stops for many people. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:18.560
answer isn’t wrong, per say, but at the same&nbsp;
time, there’s so much more going on here.
&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:25.080
If you were to go and buy a state of the art gpu,&nbsp;
they list their performance these days in TFLOPS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.760
that’s an absurd amount of computing power,&nbsp;
so why does just reducing the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.760
vertices in the scene make such an outsized&nbsp;
difference? There’s more to the story.
&nbsp;

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:39.400
Back in the 90’s, GPU’s had what was&nbsp;
called a “fixed function pipeline”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:45.000
which meant that the various stages of the GPU&nbsp;
were set in stone. The hardware had different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.400
units allocated to different tasks
What this meant practically was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.280
resources weren’t shared, it was more like&nbsp;
an assembly line with things going in 1 end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.280 --> 00:04:00.160
going through a series of fixed steps, and then&nbsp;
coming out fully processed on the other end.
&nbsp;

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:06.640
This all changed in around 2004, when AMD,&nbsp;
or ATI at the time, for those of us who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:13.720
been doing this for a long time now, developed&nbsp;
the Xenos GPU for the XBox 360. This would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:18.720
one of the earliest designs that unified the&nbsp;
shader architecture, meaning that the shader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:25.240
processing units of the GPU were shared between&nbsp;
vertex and pixel shading duties. Nvidia followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:31.120
not too long after, I think it was around&nbsp;
2006 with the Tesla generation of GPUs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.960
their first unified shader architecture.
What this means is that the GPU can now perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:42.000
sort of load balancing between the demands&nbsp;
of different types of processing. The GPU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.400
is free to dynamically allocate resources&nbsp;
between say, vertex and pixel processing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:53.600
leading to more efficient GPU utilization.&nbsp;
Whereas before with the fixed function pipeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:59.800
it couldn’t. So with that in mind, my uber&nbsp;
powerful fancy new GPU, realistically shouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:05.400
be bothered too much by the introduction of&nbsp;
even millions of extra vertices, because doing 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:12.560
fullscreen pass would be a few million pixels, but&nbsp;
wouldn’t even make a small dent in performance. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:18.360
vertices and pixels are basically shared now on&nbsp;
the hardware, there shouldn’t be a difference.
&nbsp;

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:23.840
Let’s look at this example, I have this fullscreen&nbsp;
effect going, meaning that I have an expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:29.080
fragment shader running on the whole screen,&nbsp;
every single pixel. I could be running anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:34.600
this could be changing the colour, doing various&nbsp;
blurs, in reality there’s so many things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:40.480
modern games do as post effects that the exact&nbsp;
contents of this shader isn’t that important.
&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:46.040
The ONLY important point here is that we’re&nbsp;
running at 100 fps. Now, let’s work through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:51.960
some numbers together, to get a sense of the scale&nbsp;
of work the GPU is currently doing. So this screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.960 --> 00:06:01.720
that I’m recording on is 1920x1080, so 1920&nbsp;
multiplied by 1080 is 2073600 pixels in total,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:07.360
or let’s round that to a cool 2M pixels, and I’m&nbsp;
doing a tonne of work in the pixel shader.
&nbsp;

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:12.960
Here is the vertex shader, it’s incredibly bare,&nbsp;
there’s barely any work in here, in fact the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:19.240
fragment or pixel shader is doing a magnitude of&nbsp;
work more than this. Buittttt if I do something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:25.880
really dumb, just to illustrate a point, and make&nbsp;
a buttload of triangles, in fact 1 quad for every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:32.120
pixel on the screen, so there’s 2M pixels, that&nbsp;
means that we’ve got around 4M triangles. Despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:38.320
the awesome power of this GPU, the framerate&nbsp;
drops catastrophically. It absolutely just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:44.040
grinds to a crawl, so why is it that the raw&nbsp;
power of our GPU doesn’t translate to handling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:51.000
these 4M triangles effortlessly? Something else&nbsp;
is going on here, let’s unravel this further.
&nbsp;

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:56.560
If we’re looking at a single quad, that’s composed&nbsp;
of 2 triangles which are themselves composed of 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:02.200
vertices, it’s not a whole lot. If we expand that&nbsp;
out to a full screen of quads, we get somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:08.720
in the ballpark of 4M triangles, and thus roughly&nbsp;
4M vertices in total. If you think about a screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:16.000
having 2M pixels already, and we’re able to do&nbsp;
many many many fullscreen passes, and all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.640
the associated shader computation that comes with&nbsp;
it, that number of vertices isn’t impressive.
&nbsp;

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:26.800
We can try out a few different numbers, to&nbsp;
see how this affects things. We already know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:31.640
that a single quad is pretty quick, but what&nbsp;
happens as we increase the number of quads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:36.400
and thus the number of triangles and vertices?
We can start out by just doubling the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.840
quads used, and we don’t see much in the way&nbsp;
of differences in performance. If anything is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:48.600
happening, it’s pretty minor at best. Butttt&nbsp;
as we scale the count up aggressively, as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:54.360
make the size of the quads smaller, and thus the&nbsp;
# of quads needed to fill up the screen increases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:59.560
effectively increasing the triangle density&nbsp;
on the screen, we start to notice a dropoff in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:05.400
performance. Initially, it’s nothing crazy, but&nbsp;
what we see is that it’s not a gradual decline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:11.600
there’s a real dropoff at some point.
Between tiles roughly sized 4x4, that’s 4 pixels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:19.200
high, 4 pixels wide, and then 2x2, we see this&nbsp;
absolutely catastrophic dropoff in performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:24.600
it’s just bananas how bad this gets.
Let’s talk a bit about how GPU’s work to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:30.080
better understand why this is failing so badly.&nbsp;
There’s this really great post by Nvidia called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:35.320
“Life of a Triangle - Nvidia’s Logical Pipeline”&nbsp;
that kinda gives you a behind the scenes look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:40.600
at what happens between that API call and&nbsp;
when things finally appear on the screen.
&nbsp;

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:46.200
AMD also has a really great talk entitled “ALL&nbsp;
THE PIPELINES – JOURNEY THROUGH THE GPU“ which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:50.440
has a nice overview of the different stages&nbsp;
that basically happen between you attempting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:56.120
to draw something, and pixels appearing on the&nbsp;
screen. We’ll loosely follow along with Nvidia’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:59.200
but I’ll provide a link to the&nbsp;
AMD one in the description.
&nbsp;

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:04.840
So if we imagine things starting out in our&nbsp;
own code, we’ll initiate the whole thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:10.640
with some sort of draw call. In webgl this&nbsp;
might look something like gl.drawElements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:16.440
vulkan might be something like vkCmdDrawIndexed,&nbsp;
etc. I mean the specific commands aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:22.040
important. What happens now is that the driver&nbsp;
goes ahead and validates whatever you sent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:27.080
making sure the data even makes sense, before&nbsp;
prepping it into a GPU friendly format.
&nbsp;

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:32.320
That makes it’s way over to the GPU, which does&nbsp;
some processing, let’s kinda just skim this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.400
the important takeaway here is that it&nbsp;
creates batches of triangles to work on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:42.080
and these are sent to these parts of the GPU&nbsp;
that Nvidia calls GPC’s, or graphics processing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:47.520
clusters. Let’s not wander too far off into the&nbsp;
weeds here, but let’s just mentally go with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:53.360
idea of GPC’s as being capable of handling a&nbsp;
portion of the graphics rendering independently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:59.560
from others. So obviously then, the more you have&nbsp;
of these, the better because that means your GPU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:04.720
can theoretically do more work simultaneously.
The important part at this stage is that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:10.000
will grab the vertex data for the triangles,&nbsp;
and begin scheduling work for each vertex.
&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:14.600
It’ll do the vertex processing at this point,&nbsp;
and the Nvidia doc goes over various tricks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:19.920
and optimizations they use, which aren’t&nbsp;
super important for the sake of simplicity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:26.000
In the AMD talk, they specifically refer to&nbsp;
this as the Primitive Assembler (PA) stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:32.200
whose job is to put together a triangle and then&nbsp;
forward that on to the rasterization stage.
&nbsp;

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:35.840
This is a bit like a step in an assembly&nbsp;
line that takes a bunch of pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:40.880
the individual vertices, and builds your&nbsp;
primitives for you, usually triangles.
&nbsp;

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:45.480
The next stage basically involves figuring out&nbsp;
who’s going to be doing the fragment shader work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:49.920
so at a coarse level they’re kinda testing&nbsp;
the triangle against quads on the screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:55.040
and divvying up the work. You can see on&nbsp;
Nvidia’s, depending on the screen rectangles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:00.480
they’re handing off the work to different GPC’s,&nbsp;
and AMD’s has a similar note about doing some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:05.680
scan conversion to test triangle overlap.
The end result is a bunch of fragment or pixel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:11.400
shader work is getting queued up, so what it&nbsp;
does here is it gets these 2x2 quads of pixels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:17.440
that’s 4 pixels per quad, and queues those&nbsp;
up together, you can see both Nvidia and AMD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:23.080
both reference this, and the simple reason is&nbsp;
that at that size, they can do some efficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:29.080
calculations. Stuff like texture gradients for&nbsp;
mip mapping, well a 2x2 quad has just enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:33.480
information to do that, while not being more&nbsp;
complex to implement from a hardware point of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:38.480
view. So pretty much everyone will do this.
At this point, there’s a bit more work to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:43.160
depth testing, blending, or various other&nbsp;
operations before finally getting output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:48.080
and that’s how pixels are born.
One obvious question that falls out of this is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:54.360
if 2x2 quads of pixels are the smallest&nbsp;
possible unit the GPU works with, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:59.800
happens if I draw a triangle smaller than that?
Good question, and this is where you’re realizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:05.120
that GPU’s are basically built with, you could&nbsp;
say that it’s an assumption about what you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:09.800
going to draw. And that’s pretty reasonable,&nbsp;
hardware engineers are looking for ways to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:15.680
maximize the GPU’s potential and then teach you&nbsp;
what to do and what not to do. So basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:20.760
there’s an assumption that there’s a given&nbsp;
ratio between triangle size and pixels covered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:25.320
and at some point past this, they’ll still&nbsp;
support whatever it is you’re trying to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:30.400
but in a very disapproving way.
When you try to draw something smaller, the GPU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:36.160
absolutely can still do it, but what happens is&nbsp;
that you’ve got this 2x2 quad, which is kinda like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:42.280
the smallest unit the GPU works with. The triangle&nbsp;
inhabits this quad here, what happens to these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:48.600
other 3? The answer is that the GPU still does the&nbsp;
work, but then it just throws away the result.
&nbsp;

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:55.280
In fact, it’s always doing this, just most of the&nbsp;
time, you don’t notice it. In this case, it threw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.280 --> 00:13:01.400
away 75% of the work, but let’s imagine that we&nbsp;
have a bigger triangle, so let’s look at a bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:06.000
screen here, and then we’ve got a triangle that’s&nbsp;
being rendered, so it’s touching a bunch of pixels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:11.920
right? Except we now know that the GPU works with&nbsp;
quads, so it’s actually touching a whole buncha&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:17.440
quads, some of which lie right on the edges of&nbsp;
the triangle. All of these quads here that lie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:23.480
on the edge of the triangle, they have pixels that&nbsp;
lie within the triangle, and some that don’t. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:28.760
the GPU will perform the work for the entire quad&nbsp;
each time. There’s not much you can do about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:34.520
it’s a natural consequence of rendering. Some&nbsp;
of this work, the GPU is just going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:38.840
to throw it away, it’ll be wasted.
But what you CAN affect is what kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:44.400
triangles you feed to the GPU. Really small&nbsp;
triangles are an obvious terrible case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:50.640
you get really poor quad utilisation, throwing&nbsp;
away obscene amounts of GPU performance.
&nbsp;

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:55.760
But we can go a bit further and examine&nbsp;
different topologies for a mesh. By topology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.760 --> 00:14:01.760
I mean the way the vertices are arranged to form&nbsp;
the mesh. You can triangulate a mesh in many ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:06.400
and it’s important to understand that they’re&nbsp;
not all treated the same by the GPU.
&nbsp;

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:11.600
Emil Persson, a senior graphics engineer&nbsp;
at Epic Games, known extremely well within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:16.360
graphics circles as humus, has a great&nbsp;
blog which I’ve referenced in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:21.280
for example in the last video I talked about&nbsp;
occlusion systems and one point I made was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:26.760
based on a talk he did for Avalanche games
Anyway, there’s a nice article talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:32.200
how the topology of a mesh affects the&nbsp;
performance. What the article goes into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:38.280
detail is examining how different triangulations&nbsp;
of the same mesh result in different performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:43.600
while things like the mesh’s area, or other&nbsp;
things like # of triangles are kept constant.
&nbsp;

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:48.960
The findings shouldn’t surprise us, as we can&nbsp;
see, what the test did was to increase the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:55.160
number of vertices and gauge performance. We can&nbsp;
see that the max area approach worked out best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.160 --> 00:15:01.160
while making ever thinner triangles did poorly,&nbsp;
and you already know why, because you end up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:06.480
poor quad utilization, and thus the gpu does&nbsp;
a lot of work that it has to throw away.
&nbsp;

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:12.040
As Emil’s research showed, the specific way that&nbsp;
a mesh is triangulated has a very direct impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:16.840
on performance, and better triangulation&nbsp;
can lead to better quad utilization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:21.960
and thus less wasted work from the GPU.
So given that understanding, what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:27.040
now if instead of 1 quad per pixel, what&nbsp;
happens if we push things further? let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:32.400
start upping the number, and we’ll go to&nbsp;
4 quads per pixel, or 9 quads per pixel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:37.360
or even 16 quads per pixel, or whatever
And what you’ll see is that the framerate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:42.800
will start to tank further and further. It’s&nbsp;
a lot of triangles, but not THAT many. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:50.600
2M to start, and grows to 8M, 18M, 32M
This was an interesting topic of exploration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:56.840
on g-truc’s blog, who was looking at the effect&nbsp;
of subpixel triangles on performance. As they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.840 --> 00:16:02.800
noted near the bottom, as you cranked the # of&nbsp;
vertices up, the framerate absolutely tanks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:06.880
even if you’re rendering to lower&nbsp;
resolution. This post is quite old now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:12.520
and I don’t have access to the exact setup that&nbsp;
was used there, so my numbers don’t match exactly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:17.280
but I am seeing terrible performance,&nbsp;
which is realistically all that matters.
&nbsp;

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:22.360
This goes back to the idea that your GPU is this&nbsp;
super powerful monster that can take on anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:27.160
and a buncha triangles, in the grand&nbsp;
scheme of things, isn’t that much. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:32.960
then what’s behind this drop in performance?
Let’s revisit that primitive assembly stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:38.640
but this time through the lens of AMD’s RDNA&nbsp;
architecture, as detailed by their ‘Journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:44.680
through the GPU’ talk. We touched on that earlier,&nbsp;
where we can think of Primitive Assembly like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:49.760
step in an assembly line that takes a bunch of&nbsp;
pieces, the individual vertices, and builds your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:55.680
primitives for you, usually triangles.
So on slide 19, on the RDNA architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:58.480
we can see that various parts of&nbsp;
the pipeline have been labelled
&nbsp;

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:02.880
And there’s this one here, called the&nbsp;
Primitive Assembler, which as we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:08.200
is important for assembling vertices into a&nbsp;
triangle and then outputs it to the next stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:13.080
which is often called rasterization.
This is where it gets interesting. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:17.720
this will obviously vary from architecture to&nbsp;
architecture, I mean you can go and find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:24.280
information sometimes. For example, we can look&nbsp;
at the RDNA architecture whitepaper from AMD, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:29.960
goes into some details on how the Navi class gpu’s&nbsp;
work. There’s a lot of info in here, but we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:35.120
kinda just focus on what we want from here which&nbsp;
is some info about the primitive assembly.
&nbsp;

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:39.960
Specifically, they talk about how they scale the&nbsp;
architecture, so as we can see they have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:48.200
detailed breakdown of how the Navi class GPU’s,&nbsp;
like the Radeon RX 5700 XT which came out in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:53.080
we can see how they’re structured. They&nbsp;
consist of what are called, shader engines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:59.600
the Radeon RX 5700 XT has 2 of these, and&nbsp;
within a shader engine, they’re further divided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:06.040
into shader arrays, which contain, among other&nbsp;
things, a primitive unit and a rasterizer.
&nbsp;

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:10.280
This is analogous to the Nvidia setup we&nbsp;
saw earlier, where they partitioned their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:15.240
GPU’s into what were called GPC’s&nbsp;
or graphics processing clusters.
&nbsp;

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:20.240
So the neat thing is then, you can connect that to&nbsp;
this section later in the whitepaper, where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:25.240
talk about how the primitive units assemble&nbsp;
triangles. Let’s just read this directly:
&nbsp;

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:29.160
The primitive units assemble triangles&nbsp;
from vertices and are also responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:34.440
for fixed-function tessellation. Each primitive&nbsp;
unit has been enhanced and supports culling up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:39.760
to two primitives per clock, twice as fast&nbsp;
as the prior generation. One primitive per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:44.360
clock is output to the rasterizer.
So each primitive unit can output 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:50.080
primitive per clock. So while the GPU may&nbsp;
be exceedingly powerful in some respects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:55.360
for example pixel processing, it’s still bound&nbsp;
by the rate the primitive assembler can pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.360 --> 00:19:00.080
things off to the rasterization stage.
So that’s neat, because it let’s you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:05.680
more specifically what the upper limit is, for a&nbsp;
given architecture, on how fast these things can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:12.400
just feed triangles to the rasterization stage.&nbsp;
This is going to vary wildly from gpu to gpu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:17.760
architecture to architecture, maybe a good idea&nbsp;
if you’re working on a console and have some hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:23.440
numbers to work against, but personally I think&nbsp;
it’s just best to get the broad strokes idea.
&nbsp;

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:27.800
By throwing more and more vertices, even&nbsp;
if they’re not visible, you’re forcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:32.040
them through primitive assembly, which may&nbsp;
kinda bottleneck things depending on the card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:37.560
you’re using. Slightly older cards especially&nbsp;
couldn’t output that many primitives per clock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:42.560
and so you’ll end up starving later stages&nbsp;
since this is a bit like an assembly line.
&nbsp;

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:48.320
As g-truc points out on their blog, let’s say&nbsp;
you’re dumping out a zillion small triangles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:52.160
you’re not making the most out of the&nbsp;
rasterization stage that follows it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:55.920
In fact, a tonne of performance is&nbsp;
getting flushed down the toilet.
&nbsp;

00:19:55.920 --> 00:20:00.600
So now we have all the tools and foundation&nbsp;
to go back to this simple optimization that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:06.440
we introduced in the beginning, but understand at&nbsp;
a much deeper level what’s actually happening.
&nbsp;

00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:10.920
Whatever your preconceptions of why level&nbsp;
of detail worked, now you understand that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:15.520
the GPU really isn’t all that good at&nbsp;
rendering a bunch of micro triangles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:20.680
and spends a disproportionate amount of computing&nbsp;
power doing it. Computing power that COULD have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:26.560
been used to make other stuff look more awesome.&nbsp;
We also now understand that we’re also inundating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:33.080
the GPU with useless small triangles, which&nbsp;
beyond all that 2x2 quad stuff, you’re forcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:38.600
this to go through primitive assembly.
A few years ago, Unreal unveiled Nanite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:43.680
a really interesting new technology that I’m&nbsp;
sure you’ve heard of by now. But if you haven’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:48.680
no worries, Unreal’s Nanite is, in&nbsp;
essence, a continuous and automatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:55.200
level of detail system. No need for artists to&nbsp;
do this, Unreal handles it automagically.
&nbsp;

00:20:55.200 --> 00:21:00.480
We can look through their Siggraph 2021&nbsp;
presentation entitled Nanite, a Deep Dive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:05.680
and one interesting thing that pops up is they&nbsp;
confirm a lot of what we’ve talked about here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:12.240
On this page, about 80 pages in, this is a VERY&nbsp;
long presentation, they talk about some technical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:17.960
decisions they made. We can see here that they&nbsp;
mention just how crappy tiny triangles are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:23.680
and they point out that gpu’s are built with&nbsp;
parallel pixels in mind, but not triangles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:28.040
which jives with what we’ve seen. I mean, they’re&nbsp;
the Epic team, they’ve done their homework.
&nbsp;

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:34.320
As they mention in their slides, a lot of&nbsp;
modern GPU’s can setup 4 triangles/clock max,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:39.840
so a primitive assembly bottleneck is a very&nbsp;
real problem that they set out to solve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:46.080
or at least work around. So what they did was&nbsp;
write a software rasterizer for micro triangles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:50.120
and that ended up being considerably&nbsp;
faster. We don’t have to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:55.800
the details of their implementation, but now&nbsp;
AT LEAST we understand their motivation.
&nbsp;

